  comparisonTitle: "The same insertion order with JavaScript Set",
  comparisonBefore: "JavaScript Set also keeps insertion order. Here a copied Set is updated so that the original remains available for the last check. Array.from copies its members to an array.",
  comparisonAfter: "Both conversions yield an array. JavaScript delete and add mutate the copied Set, whereas Seseragi remove and insert return new Set values.",
  detailTitle: "Order, equality, and conversion cost",
  details: [
    "toArray accepts Set<A> and returns Array<A>. It does not remove duplicates itself: the input is already a Set. Eq supplies equality and Hash supplies compatible key hashes for the construction and update operations; this conversion does not require a new comparison.",
    "Insertion order is part of the Set contract. Reinserting an existing member keeps its position; removing it before reinsertion puts it at the end. An empty Set produces an empty result. Conversion takes work proportional to the number of members under the documented collection cost model."
